<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Muang Phuan</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1899 CE (approx.) · capital Xieng Khouang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tai Phuan principality on the Plain of Jars in north-eastern Laos, caught for centuries between Lan Xang, Vietnam and Siam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Muang Phuan, centred on Xieng Khouang, emerged among the Tai Phuan people of the high plateau; Lao chronicles link its ruling house to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khun-borom">Khun Borom</a> and date its founding to the 14th century, when it was brought into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang</a>. The plateau is famous for the much older stone jars of the Plain of Jars.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers governed a strategic upland crossroads between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> valley and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>, paying tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang</a> and later to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-vientiane">Vientiane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Phrabang</a>, while often sending tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Dai Viet</a> as well. Buddhist temples and stupas filled the capital. Its position made it a frequent battlefield and source of forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Siam deported much of the Phuan population across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> in the 1830s, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty</a> briefly annexed Xieng Khouang as Tran Ninh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haw-wars">Chinese Haw</a> bands ravaged it in the 1870s. Under the French it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, and its ruler lost power by 1899.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/muang-phuan.jpg" alt="Muang Phuan" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Eynaud · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laos_-_Division_territ%C3%B2riala_v%C3%A8rs_1750_(vuege).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xieng Khouang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tai Phuan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xieng Khouang, Principality of Phuan, Tran Ninh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=muang-phuan" aria-label="Muang Phuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7468 1457 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Muang Phuan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7788 1713L7756 1697M7788 1713L7784 1767M7788 1713L7930 1822M7788 1713L7784 1767"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7756 1697h0M7784 1767h0M7930 1822h0M7784 1767h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7788 1713h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7788 1713h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=muang-phuan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
